C/ From PDF p. 239 of "Programming in HAL/S".

  STARTUP:
  PROGRAM;
     DECLARE ORBIT EVENT LATCHED;
     DECLARE ORBIT2 EVENT LATCHED;
     DECLARE ORBIT3 EVENT;
     DECLARE I INTEGER;
     
  GUIDANCE:
  TASK;
        I = 1;
  CLOSE GUIDANCE;
  
  NAVIGATION:
  TASK;
        I = 2;
  CLOSE NAVIGATION;
  
  CONTROL:
  TASK;
        I = 3;
  CLOSE CONTROL;
  
  FREEFALL:
  TASK;
        I = 4;
  CLOSE FREEFALL;
  
  FREEFALL2:
  TASK;
        I = 5;
  CLOSE FREEFALL2;
  
     SCHEDULE NAVIGATION PRIORITY(60), REPEAT EVERY 1.0;
     SCHEDULE GUIDANCE PRIORITY(70), REPEAT EVERY 1 / 6 UNTIL ORBIT;
     SCHEDULE CONTROL PRIORITY(80), REPEAT EVERY 1 / 20 UNTIL ORBIT;
C    HAL/S-FC had a bug (relative to HAL/S documentation) and
C    could not generate object code for an event expression like
C    the following:
C    SCHEDULE FREEFALL ON (ORBIT & NOT GUIDANCE & NOT CONTROL) 
C       PRIORITY(75), REPEAT EVERY 1 / 10;
C    hence I've arbitrarily replaced it with a different example
C    below:
     SCHEDULE FREEFALL ON (ORBIT & (ORBIT2 & ORBIT3)) 
        PRIORITY(75), REPEAT EVERY 1 / 10;
C/ USA003087 p.166 (13-7): PRIORITY's legal range is implementation-
C/ dependent; PRIORITY(776) was this corpus's own fabricated addition
C/ (not from the book), so its exact value has no significance beyond
C/ "distinct and high" -- substituted with 240 (this implementation's
C/ legal range is 0-255), which doesn't collide with 60/70/80/75 above.
     SCHEDULE FREEFALL2 ON (ORBIT | (ORBIT2 | ORBIT3))
        PRIORITY(240), REPEAT EVERY 1 / 10;
     WRITE(6) 'ALL TASKS SCHEDULED';
Y %SVCI(0);
  CLOSE STARTUP;
